                "The foremost contractual mechanism governing deal certainty is the Breakup Fee (or Termination Fee). Breakup fees are negotiated in two directions: Target Breakup Fees and Reverse Termination Fees. A Target Breakup Fee is payable by the target company to the acquirer if the target board walks away from the signed agreement to accept a superior unsolicited bid from a rival suitor (a 'Topping Bid'). Under Delaware corporate case law (the governing standard for US corporate governance), target breakup fees are legally constrained to reasonable levels—typically between 2.0% and 4.0% of transaction equity value. If an acquirer demands an excessively punitive breakup fee (e.g., 8.0%), Delaware courts will strike down the provision as an illegal 'deal lock-up' that unconstitutionally coerces shareholders and deters superior bids.",
